.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt CONNECT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm connect
.Nd connect a socket to a peer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn connect "int fd" "const struct sockaddr *addr" "socklen_t addrlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn connect
sets the peer of socket
.Fa fd
from
.Fa addr .
.Fa addrlen
is the address length in bytes.
For an IPv4 peer, the kernel reads the first eight bytes of a
.Vt sockaddr_in :
the family, port, and address.
The port and address are in network byte order.
The operation is subcall 3 of i386
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
system call 102.
.Ss Stream sockets
On a
.Dv SOCK_STREAM
socket, the call starts the TCP handshake.
A previously bound local port is retained; otherwise an unused ephemeral
port from 49152 through 65535 is selected.
A duplicate pairing of local port, peer address, and peer port is refused.
.Pp
A blocking socket waits until the handshake completes or fails.
The call succeeds even if the peer's FIN arrives before the caller resumes.
The connection is then in
.Dv CLOSE_WAIT ;
a read returns any queued data followed by EOF.
A pending signal that is neither blocked nor ignored interrupts the wait
with
.Er EINTR .
With
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
the call returns 0 if the connection is already established or
.Er EINPROGRESS
otherwise.
The caller can wait for completion with
.Xr poll 2
and read
.Dv SO_ERROR
with
.Xr getsockopt 2 .
Reading
.Dv SO_ERROR
clears the pending error.
.Pp
A second connect on a socket still in
.Dv SYN_SENT
fails with
.Er EALREADY .
A listening socket, or one with any other attached TCP control block,
gives
.Er EISCONN ;
this includes a control block left closed after failure.
Close the descriptor and create a new socket for another attempt.
.Pp
The initial retransmission timeout is one second.
It doubles on timeout to a maximum of 60 seconds.
Seven successive expiries permit retransmission; the eighth ends the
connection.
A failed blocking handshake returns
.Er ECONNREFUSED ,
including a handshake that timed out.
The pending socket error distinguishes a timeout
.Pq Er ETIMEDOUT
from a reset during active open
.Pq Er ECONNREFUSED .
During the active open a reset counts only when it acknowledges the SYN,
its acknowledgement number being the SYN's sequence number plus one;
any other reset is ignored.
After establishment a reset at the next expected sequence number records
.Er ECONNRESET .
A reset elsewhere in the receive window is answered with an
acknowledgement and leaves the connection open, since the true peer
answers that acknowledgement with a reset at the expected number;
a reset outside the window is ignored.
This is RFC 5961, section 3.2.
These pending errors are reported by
.Dv SO_ERROR
and cause
.Dv POLLERR .
.Pp
The initial sequence number is the tick count times 2500, the
4-microsecond clock of RFC 6528, plus the low 32 bits of SipHash-2-4 of
the local address, remote address, local port and remote port, under a
key drawn from the kernel's random generator at the first
.Fn listen
or
.Fn connect
after boot.
.Ss Datagram sockets
On an IPv4
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM
socket, the call records a destination for sends and restricts received
datagrams to that peer.
There is no handshake or wait.
An unbound socket acquires an ephemeral local port.
Another connect replaces the peer.
.Pp
An address with family
.Dv AF_UNSPEC
disconnects a datagram socket and leaves its local binding in place.
Only the two-byte family is needed for this request.
A stream socket cannot be disconnected this way.
Raw sockets and packet sockets do not support
.Fn connect .
.Ss Local connections
A destination in 127/8 is replaced with the current local IPv4 address.
There is no separate
.Li lo
interface.
Self-addressed packets are queued for delivery at system call exit or
by the timer; they do not require a network peer.
Address queries report the canonical local address, not 127.0.0.1.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn connect
returns 0 on success.
On failure, including a nonblocking connection in progress, it returns
-1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
connects to its own TCP listener through 127.0.0.1, exchanges data in
both directions, and checks the reported peer address.
It also connects to a closed local port and requires
.Er ECONNREFUSED .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EADDRNOTAVAIL]"
.It Bq Er EADDRINUSE
The bound local port is already paired with the same peer address and port.
.It Bq Er EADDRNOTAVAIL
No ephemeral TCP port is available.
.It Bq Er EAFNOSUPPORT
A datagram address has a family other than
.Dv AF_INET
or
.Dv AF_UNSPEC ,
or a stream socket was given
.Dv AF_UNSPEC .
The stream handler does not reject other nonzero family values before
interpreting the address as IPv4.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
An unbound datagram socket could not acquire an ephemeral binding.
.It Bq Er EALREADY
A TCP handshake is already in progress in
.Dv SYN_SENT .
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er ECONNREFUSED
The blocking TCP handshake ended without an established connection.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The address, or the socketcall argument array, cannot be read from user memory.
.It Bq Er EINPROGRESS
A nonblocking TCP handshake has started but is not yet established.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal interrupted the blocking wait.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa addrlen
is less than two bytes, or less than eight for a nonzero address family.
.It Bq Er EISCONN
The stream socket is listening or already has a TCP control block in
a state other than
.Dv SYN_SENT .
.It Bq Er ENOBUFS
A TCP control block could not be allocated.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
does not refer to a socket.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
The socket is raw, belongs to
.Dv AF_PACKET ,
or is neither a supported datagram nor stream socket.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr getsockopt 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
